1. Introduction
Morus alba L. belongs to the Moraceae family. It is a deciduous shrub or tree that is grown naturally,
and is widely cultivated for medicinal and edible purposes. Four M. alba parts have been used
medicinally, as registered in the Korean pharmacopeias, including the twigs (Sang-Ji, Mori Ramulus),
leaves (Sang-Yeop, Mori Folium), fruits (Sang-Sim-Ja, Mori Fructus), and root barks (Sanb-Baek-Pi,
Mori Radicis Cortex), each of which have diﬀerent therapeutic eﬃcacies [1,2]. Each medicinal M. alba

